CSS math over a percentage of a layout length: calc() of one percentage and lengths, and min()/max()/clamp() of
two or three terms of that form (`width:calc(100% - 20px)`, `max-width:min(260px, 100%)`). The host folds such math
to the canonical `bbl-calc(<percent> <px>[ lower <percent> <px>][ upper <percent> <px>])`, which the number parser
reads for properties taking both lengths and percentages (Unit::LENGTH_CALC); a computed Percentage carries the
lengths and bounds (Style::LengthCalc, held apart in ComputedValues for the few values that have them),
ResolveValueOr resolves them against the percentage's base; sized decorator boxes (patch 0025) carry them per
axis, and transitions mix unbounded terms (bounded ones swap).
CSS Values: https://www.w3.org/TR/css-values-4/#calc-notation

